<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ashot III of Armenia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 953–<a href="/years/977">977</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Ashot III the Merciful, Ashot III of Bagratid Armenia · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagratuni-dynasty">Bagratuni dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ashot III, called the Merciful, was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagratid-armenia">Bagratid Armenia</a>. In 961 he moved the capital to Ani, which grew into a great city on the trade routes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. He gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kars">Kars</a> to his brother Mushegh in 963, and his son Gurgen founded the Kingdom of Lori, creating junior Bagratid kingdoms.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ashot-iii-of-armenia.jpg" alt="Ashot III of Armenia" width="320" height="479" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tony Bowden · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monument_knight_in_Gyumri.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ashot III was the eldest son of King Abas, and had two brothers, Atrnerseh and Mushegh. During his father's reign he held the office of Prince of Princes, which under the first four Bagratuni kings was mostly held by members of the royal house next in line to the throne. He succeeded Abas in 953. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagrationi-dynasty">Georgian Bagratids</a>, who were related to the Armenian Bagratunis by marriage, recognised his suzerainty, as did the rulers of Vaspurakan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kars">Kars</a>, Khachen, Parisos and Shirvan, and he used the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahanshah">Shahanshah</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgians">Georgians</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In the first years of his reign Ashot gathered an army of 30,000 and tried to free the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dvin">Dvin</a> from Muslim rule. He was defeated near the city, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of Dvin continued to exist in the heart of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>. Having failed there, he turned to Ani. The Bagratunis had earlier lived at Bagaran, Yerazgavors and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kars">Kars</a>, none of which had Ani's natural defences. In 961 an assembly of the princes of Armenia, the catholicos Anania I Mokatsi and many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> was held at Ani for Ashot's coronation, and the city was proclaimed capital. About 964 he built a wall across the narrowest part of its promontory, later called the Ashotyan walls, and he sponsored monasteries, hospitals, schools and almshouses.</p><p>In the late 960s and 970s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> under Nicephorus Phocas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-tzimiskes">John Tzimiskes</a> threatened Armenian lands, and Taron was seized in 968. In 974 Tzimiskes marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mus">Mush</a>, but met Ashot's army of 30,000 and left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>; Ashot then supplied him with 10,000 soldiers for his campaign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>. A council he called at Shirakavan in 968 chose Bishop Vahan of Syunik as catholicos, but Vahan leaned to the Chalcedonian church and was deposed at Ani in 969.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Ashot sent his brother Mushegh to rule at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kars">Kars</a> with the title of king, and in 966 gave the district of Dzoraget near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-sevan">Lake Sevan</a> to his son Gurgen, ancestor of the Kyurikid line. Such sub-kingdoms continued under his successors, and served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> so long as the king at Ani remained strong. His wife Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrovanuysh">Khosrovanuysh</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanahin-monastery">Sanahin Monastery</a> in 966 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haghpat-monastery">Haghpat Monastery</a> in 976. His epithet, the Merciful, is first found in documents after his death; his contemporary Anania Mokatsi does not use it, and a folk tradition from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhaltsikhe">Akhaltsikhe</a> links it to his punishing an overseer who withheld gold meant for the poor. He was probably buried at Ani or at Horomos.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ashot III of Armenia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Ashot III</b> moved the capital to Ani in 961, which grew wealthy on trade between Byzantium and Iran.</blockquote><a href="/state/bagratid-armenia">Bagratid Armenia</a></li><li><blockquote>In 963 <b>Ashot III of Bagratid Armenia</b> gave the district of Vanand, with the fortress of Kars, to his brother Mushegh, who took the title king.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kars">Kingdom of Kars</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 979 Gurgen, son of <b>Ashot III of Bagratid Armenia</b>, was given the northern provinces of Tashir and Dzoraget and took the title king, founding the Kyurikian line, named after his Georgian-style name Kyurike.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-lori">Kingdom of Lori</a></li></ul></section>
